\section{Channel order}{

A device export lists columns in \emph{device channel} order. Every other
\code{pressR} function - \code{\link[=pr_mask]{pr_mask()}}, \code{\link[=pr_calc_cop]{pr_calc_cop()}}, the heatmaps - indexes
pressure columns in column-major grid order, the order
\code{which(layout$active)} produces. For a single-panel device the two agree;
for a multi-panel mat they do not. Supplying \code{channel_map} (or a layout
that carries one, such as \code{pr_layout_saddle_novel()}) applies the single
permutation \code{pressure[, order, drop = FALSE]} that reconciles them.

\code{channel_map} may be a grid-shaped matrix whose entry at \verb{[row, col]} is
the device channel sitting at that grid cell, or the equivalent bare
integer permutation. When \code{channel_map} is \code{NULL} and \code{layout} carries a
\code{channel_order}, that order is used; otherwise the columns are left in
device order.
}

\section{Missing values}{

Pliance writes an unread cell as the literal token \verb{___}. With
\code{on_na = "drop_frame"} (the default) any frame containing one is removed
whole, which is what keeps per-frame statistics comparable across a
recording; \code{on_na = "zero"} keeps the frame and reads the cell as \code{0}.
A frame whose \emph{timestamp} is missing is always dropped, since it cannot be
placed on the time axis. The counts land in
\code{metadata$n_frames_raw} and \code{metadata$n_frames_dropped}.
}

